---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Dubai property valuation" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <ValuationTool />

  <h2>What does the written report contain?</h2>

  <p>
    Six things, each of which you can check yourself afterwards. A valuation you cannot audit
    is an opinion with a letterhead on it.
  </p>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>Comparable transactions from the last six months in your building, with dates</li>
    <li>The price per square foot those transactions imply, and your unit measured against it</li>
    <li>An adjustment for floor, aspect and finish, stated as an amount rather than a feeling</li>
    <li>The current service charge per square foot and what it does to a buyer’s offer</li>
    <li>A realistic asking price and a realistic acceptance price, which are not the same number</li>
    <li>The evidence list, so every figure above can be traced back to a recorded transaction</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    What it does not contain is a projection. Nobody publishing honestly can tell you what your
    property will be worth next year, and this market gives a specific reason not to try: H1
    2026 residential transactions in Dubai fell 13.6% year on year, and Q2 2026 saw both prices
    and rents decline. A valuation describes today, from records. That is the only claim it can
    support.
  </p>

  <h2>How is a Dubai property valuation produced?</h2>

  <p>
    Three methods, and the one you need depends entirely on who is going to read the result.
    Most owners asking what their property is worth need the first. Anyone applying for a
    golden visa, settling an estate or arguing with a bank needs the third, and the first two
    will not be accepted in its place.
  </p>

  <DataTable
    caption="The three valuation methods used in Dubai"
    columns={['Method', 'What it uses', 'When it applies', 'Typical cost']}
    rows={[
      [
        'Comparable sales',
        'Recorded transactions for similar units in the same building or community',
        'Deciding an asking price, or checking an offer against evidence',
        'Free as offered here',
      ],
      [
        'Income approach',
        'Achieved rent, occupancy and running costs, capitalised to a value',
        'A tenanted unit, or a short-let where the return is the point',
        'Varies by provider',
      ],
      [
        'DLD-certified valuation',
        'An inspection plus Land Department data, issued as a certificate',
        'Golden visa, mortgage, court, inheritance — anywhere an authority must accept it',
        'Fee set by the Dubai Land Department',
      ],
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The costs above are stated as they are known. The DLD certification fee and current
    turnaround are published by the Land Department and belong in this table as figures:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="DLD valuation certificate fee and stated turnaround" />
  </p>

  <p>
    The distinction that catches people is that a broker’s valuation and a certified valuation
    are different documents with different legal weight. A broker’s valuation is a commercial
    opinion, however well evidenced. A DLD-certified valuation is issued through the Land
    Department and is the one an authority will act on. If someone offers you a “certified”
    valuation without the Land Department involved, that is not what the word means here.
  </p>

  <p>
    How each method is carried out, step by step, is set out in{' '}
    <a href="/property-valuation-dubai/how-it-works/">how Dubai property valuation works</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who carries out the valuation?</h2>

  <PartnerCard />

  <p>
    This matters more than a disclosure paragraph usually does. Valuing a property for a
    transaction is a licensed activity in Dubai. Plainly explains how valuation works, what it
    costs and what the methods are, which is information and requires no licence. The valuation
    itself is performed by the licensed partner above, and the registration number shown is
    theirs.
  </p>

  <h2>When do you need a DLD-certified valuation?</h2>

  <p>
    For the golden visa, the property must be valued at AED 2 million or more, and the
    valuation has to be DLD-certified. An estate agent’s appraisal will not be accepted, and
    neither will the price you paid. The threshold applies to the certified valuation figure,
    which is why owners close to the line get the certificate before making any other
    arrangements.
  </p>

  <p>
    What counts toward the AED 2 million, what does not, and how the certificate is issued are
    covered in full on{' '}
    <a href="/property-valuation-dubai/golden-visa/">golden visa property valuation in Dubai</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What moves a Dubai valuation most?</h2>

  <p>
    Floor level and aspect, first. Two identical layouts in the same tower can transact well
    apart on view alone, and the transaction record shows it if you read unit by unit rather
    than taking a building average.
  </p>

  <p>
    Service charge, second, and it is the one owners consistently underweight. A buyer prices
    the annual service charge into what they will pay, so a high charge per square foot depresses
    the achievable price even where the unit itself is identical to a cheaper-to-run comparable
    down the road. Published service charge figures per community belong here as evidence:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="service charge per sq ft by community, from the DLD service charge index" />
  </p>

  <p>
    Third, condition at handover. A unit that went through snagging and had its defects fixed
    inside the developer’s liability window presents differently to one that did not, and the
    difference is visible to a buyer.{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/">Snagging in Dubai</a> covers what that inspection costs and what
    it catches.
  </p>

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />

  <RequestForm />
</PageShell>
